snippet.textDisplay:
@devils_advocate_88 It’s definitely not impossible from a skilled cuber, but this specific trick of matching all sides was performed by other magicians like Steven Brundage with sleight of hand swapping the cube (even though he is also a cuber). It’s just less hassle and risk since you don’t know how crazy their shuffle will be.

Also it’s interesting you mention blindfolded cubing. Technically, they aren’t memorizing the 3D composition of the cube and then solving it in their head as if they would looking at it. They’re just memorizing the order in which to solve pieces and performing algorithms to match that order. For example, they would memorize a string of letters where each letter corresponds to a position on the cube. Then they just need to perform known algorithms to move each piece to the next letter position.

Sorry I rambled, I just really like blind cubing.

